From 89e64234ea31a7d5002852a9d2316b525e469a98 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 15:51:52 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(procurement): the specification the study implies, and a
 deploy script that cannot rotate a key
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Patch 0015.

THE DEPLOY SCRIPT ROTATED THE PRODUCTION KEY AGAIN

Patch 0014 claimed to fix this. It did not. The new version grepped
`fly secrets list` to decide whether a secret existed; the grep did not match
flyctl's actual output, detection silently returned false, and the next deploy
minted a fresh GRIDFORGE_API_KEYS over a key set ninety seconds earlier. Same bug,
one extra step.

Detection is no longer load-bearing. Minting is opt-in (--bootstrap), and when the
secret list cannot be read the script REFUSES to write rather than guessing —
uncertainty must never resolve to overwriting a credential. Detection itself now
prefers `--json` and falls back to a whitespace-tolerant parse.

And tests/test_deploy_script.py runs the script against a fake `fly` that records
every argv, asserting it never calls `secrets set` on a secret that exists —
including from the exact indented table that defeated the previous version. A
shell script with no test is how a bug survives the patch that claims to fix it.

THE PROCUREMENT SPECIFICATION (EUR 18,000)

A study ends with "the transformer sets the date at 52 weeks". The client then has
to buy a transformer, and between the finding and the purchase order sits three
weeks of an engineer writing a specification and comparing four quotations that
answer four different questions. That gap is the product.

  * Every numeric requirement is derived from the capacity model and names the
    constraint it came from. validate() refuses a mandatory numeric requirement
    with nothing behind it: a number nobody can trace is a number somebody
    invented, and those are how a tender specifies equipment the hall does not
    need.
  * Duties are sized for the hall's END STATE, not the rung's delta. A rung often
    unlocks three racks; nobody buys a transformer for three racks. Sizing to the
    delta gives a specification that is traceable and absurd.
  * Duties are quoted at the SITE's conditions. A CDU rated at a 5 K approach and
    installed on a 24 K site is the commonest way a liquid retrofit
    under-delivers, so the clause says so in capitals and asks for the selection
    output at our conditions.
  * No make, no model, no supplier named. We state duty and interfaces and take no
    margin on hardware — which is why a client can hand this to their own
    procurement without a conflict to declare. A test asserts no manufacturer name
    appears anywhere in the document.
  * Bids are compared in racks and weeks. 35 compliance / 30 programme / 20 price
    / 15 judged. The bid 12% cheaper and 14 weeks slower is the expensive one, and
    a non-compliant bid never outranks a compliant one whatever its price.
  * The 15 judged points are DECLARED UNSCORED rather than computed. A tool that
    filled that column would be inventing the part that needs an engineer.

THE FLYWHEEL — WHY IT IS WORTH SELLING UNDER THE STUDY

A study rests on library defaults: E0, a -50%/+100% band, AACE Class 5. A returned
bid is a dated, attributable, project-specific price. `bids --ingest` prints the
exact `cost add` commands it implies, and the moment that line lands, every future
study touching it gets stronger and its accuracy class improves. Procurement work
pays twice.

The conversion is the dangerous part and is tested hardest. A supplier quotes one
delivered number; the library holds tapoff.unit per rack, ups.per_kW per kW and
busway.replacement as a lump sum. A lump sum entered on a per-rack line is wrong by
the rack count, carries a QUOTATION's evidence class, and every future study
inherits it with a straight face — worse than no flywheel at all. The basis comes
from costs.declared_unit(), a registry populated by the constraint that prices each
key, not guessed from a step's already-multiplied total.

ALSO

  * `gridforge spec --list|--constraint` and `gridforge bids --ingest`, with a
    machine-readable response schedule the two commands round-trip through.
  * /v1/spec (3 units), /v1/bids (2 units), gridforge_spec and gridforge_bids over
    MCP. API version 0.9.0.
  * /reference now publishes a complete specification and its response schedule —
    the document a prospect would actually send to suppliers, before paying.
  * The parity test now checks EVERY engagement sold on the site against the
    engine, not the two that were checked by hand.
  * SupplierResponse refuses an undated price (it cannot become a dated library
    entry) and names the field when a value is not a number, because "failed to
    load" is useless to whoever has to go back to the supplier.

TESTS

39 new (289 total). Traceability is enforced, not intended; an untraceable
requirement is refused; weights must total 100; the transformer rating is checked
against the IT load it serves; the document passes the report gates and warns
against raising a purchase order on it; a lump-sum bid converts correctly onto all
three library bases; and the deploy script is proven unable to rotate a live key.

tsc clean, next lint clean, next build clean. Reference artefacts regenerate byte
for byte.

